(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-hemostasis-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Hemostasis — Quiz 1 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Red blood cells contain abundant carbonic anhydrase. How does this enzyme support the blood's role in gas transport?","choices":["It loads oxygen onto the heme groups so that each red cell carries more O2 per pass","It speeds hydration of CO2 to carbonic acid, letting most CO2 travel as bicarbonate","It buffers lactic acid from the red cell's own anaerobic glucose metabolism","It binds free iron and keeps hemoglobin locked in its oxygen-carrying ferrous state"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: carbonic anhydrase catalyzes CO2 + H2O to H2CO3, so most CO2 is carried as HCO3-, the major extracellular buffer. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 3"},{"q":"The biconcave disc shape of the red blood cell chiefly benefits its function by...","choices":["maximizing surface area for gas exchange and allowing deformation through narrow capillaries","increasing its internal volume so that it can store a large amount of extra dissolved oxygen","providing rigidity so the cell resists rupture in high-pressure arteries","creating a nucleus-rich center that supports rapid ongoing cell division"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The biconcave geometry gives a high surface-area-to-volume ratio (short diffusion distances for O2 and CO2) and lets the cell fold to squeeze through capillaries narrower than its own diameter, both of which optimize gas transport. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 6"},{"q":"Blood makes up roughly what fraction of total body weight, and what is its largest component by volume?","choices":["about 25% of body weight, with red cells being the largest fraction","about 40% of body weight, split evenly between cells and plasma","about 7–8% of body weight, with plasma being the largest fraction by volume","under 1% of body weight, made up almost entirely of platelets in this scenario"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blood accounts for roughly 7–8% of body weight (about 5 liters in an adult), and plasma — mostly water with dissolved proteins, electrolytes, and nutrients — makes up a little over half of blood volume, the single largest component. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-6"},{"q":"A reticulocyte count is useful clinically because reticulocytes are...","choices":["platelets that have just been released from megakaryocytes","old red cells about to be removed by the spleen as it typically occurs here","a type of white cell that increases during infection","immature red cells whose number reflects the marrow's production rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reticulocytes are newly released, not-yet-mature red cells; a rising count signals that the marrow is actively producing red cells (for example, in response to blood loss), while a low count suggests impaired production. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Platelets (thrombocytes) are best described as...","choices":["mature nucleated cells that divide within the bloodstream","a specialized type of white blood cell that fights infection","cell fragments pinched off from large megakaryocytes in the marrow","oxygen-carrying cells closely related to red blood cells in most cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Platelets are not whole cells but membrane-bound fragments shed from megakaryocytes in the bone marrow; despite lacking a nucleus, they carry granules and machinery essential for hemostasis. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 6, 30-33"},{"q":"Each hemoglobin molecule can carry up to four oxygen molecules because it contains...","choices":["four globin chains but only a single shared oxygen-binding pocket overall","four heme groups, each with an iron atom that binds one oxygen","a single large heme unit that binds all four oxygens at once","four copies of the iron-free protein globin without any heme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemoglobin is a tetramer with four polypeptide (globin) chains, each holding a heme group with a central iron (Fe2+) atom; each iron can reversibly bind one oxygen molecule, so one hemoglobin carries up to four. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 20-21"},{"q":"The sigmoid (S-shaped) oxygen-hemoglobin curve reflects that...","choices":["binding of one oxygen molecule increases hemoglobin's affinity for the next","oxygen binding is entirely independent and non-cooperative at each of the four sites","each hemoglobin molecule can bind only a single oxygen at any given time","hemoglobin binds oxygen and carbon dioxide at the exact same binding site"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemoglobin binds oxygen cooperatively: attachment of one O2 changes the molecule's conformation and raises its affinity for subsequent O2, producing the characteristic S-shaped curve that favors loading in the lungs and unloading at tissues. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 21"},{"q":"Hemoglobin concentration is normally reported around 15 g/dL. This value reflects...","choices":["the number of white cells circulating per microliter","the fraction of hemoglobin currently bound to oxygen","the amount of oxygen-binding protein per volume of blood","the percentage of blood volume made up of red cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemoglobin concentration measures how much oxygen-carrying protein is present per unit volume of blood; since each gram binds about 1.34 mL of O2, this value largely determines the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 21"},{"q":"A markedly elevated hematocrit (as in polycythemia) tends to...","choices":["reduce oxygen-carrying capacity by crowding out the plasma proteins","raise blood viscosity, increasing the resistance the heart must pump against","have no effect on flow because viscosity depends only on the plasma","lower blood viscosity markedly, easing the overall workload placed on the heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Viscosity rises steeply as hematocrit climbs; thicker blood increases vascular resistance, so the heart must generate more pressure to maintain flow. This is why very high hematocrits strain the circulation. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 6"},{"q":"Each gram of hemoglobin can bind a fixed amount of oxygen, which is why hemoglobin concentration largely determines...","choices":["the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood","the number of white cells available to fight infection","the speed at which blood clots after an injury","the plasma colloid osmotic pressure of the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because each gram of hemoglobin binds about 1.34 mL of oxygen when fully saturated, the total hemoglobin concentration sets the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity; this is why anemia, with low hemoglobin, reduces oxygen delivery even when the lungs work normally. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 20-21"},{"q":"Iron absorbed from the diet is carried in the blood bound to...","choices":["bile salts, which shuttle it back to the liver in the body's tissues","hemoglobin, which ferries it through the plasma","albumin, the general carrier for all trace metals","transferrin, which delivers it to the marrow and storage sites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Absorbed iron is transported in plasma bound to transferrin, which delivers it to the bone marrow for hemoglobin synthesis and to the liver and other tissues for storage as ferritin; free iron is toxic, so it is kept protein-bound. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 17"},{"q":"The normal circulating lifespan of a red blood cell is about...","choices":["several years, being among the longest-lived cells","120 days, after which macrophages remove the aged cell","30 days, limited by the loss of their mitochondria","a few hours, similar to the lifespan of a neutrophil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Red cells survive roughly 120 days; lacking a nucleus and organelles, they cannot repair themselves and become fragile, so macrophages (mainly in the spleen and liver) phagocytize the senescent cells and recycle their components. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Most of the erythropoietin that drives red cell production is released from the...","choices":["bone marrow, whenever iron stores in the body run low","liver, in direct response to a rising red blood cell count","kidneys, in response to reduced oxygen delivery to renal tissue","spleen, as it continually breaks down senescent red blood cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The kidneys are the principal source of erythropoietin; specialized cells sense reduced oxygen delivery and secrete EPO, which stimulates marrow erythropoiesis to restore oxygen-carrying capacity. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-16"},{"q":"Deficiency of vitamin B12 or folate produces abnormally large red cells because these vitamins are required for...","choices":["DNA synthesis, so nuclear maturation lags while the cytoplasm keeps growing","hemoglobin assembly, so cells swell to hold the excess unbound globin","iron incorporation, so cells enlarge to compensate for low oxygen content","membrane repair, so cells round up and expand as they lose deformability"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: without B12/folate, thymidine and thus DNA cannot be made, so the cell grows without dividing, forming large macrocytes. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 17"},{"q":"When an old red blood cell is broken down, the heme portion is processed so that...","choices":["the iron is discarded in the urine and the heme exhaled as a general rule","it is converted directly into new hemoglobin without change","its iron is recycled while the rest becomes bilirubin excreted in bile","the entire heme molecule is stored intact for later reuse"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. As senescent red cells are destroyed, the globin is recycled to amino acids and the heme iron is salvaged for reuse, while the remaining porphyrin ring is converted to bilirubin, which the liver conjugates and excretes in bile. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Vitamin B12 and folate are needed for red cell production chiefly because they...","choices":["stimulate the kidney to release more erythropoietin under most normal conditions","form the globin chains that make up the hemoglobin molecule","provide the iron atom that sits at the center of each heme","support DNA synthesis required for the rapid division of precursor cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Erythropoiesis involves rapid cell division, which demands DNA synthesis; B12 and folate are cofactors for that process. Their deficiency impairs nuclear maturation, producing large, poorly functional cells (megaloblastic anemia). — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 17"},{"q":"Vitamin K is essential for coagulation because it...","choices":["binds calcium so it cannot participate in the cascade","directly dissolves clots once a wound has healed during this particular process in the tissues","is required by the liver to synthesize several clotting factors, including prothrombin","is the enzyme that converts fibrinogen into fibrin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin K is a cofactor the liver uses to produce functional prothrombin and factors VII, IX, and X; without it, these factors are made in an inactive form, and clotting is impaired — the basis of warfarin's action. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 46-47"},{"q":"Days after a clot forms, it is dissolved when plasminogen is converted to...","choices":["plasmin, which digests the fibrin meshwork of the clot","fibrinogen, which is redeposited to enlarge the clot","thrombin, which reinforces the fibrin meshwork further","prothrombin activator, which restarts the clotting cascade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Plasminogen incorporated into the clot is activated to plasmin, a protease that breaks down fibrin (fibrinolysis), clearing the clot once the vessel has healed. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-48"},{"q":"Thrombin does more than convert fibrinogen to fibrin; it also...","choices":["carries oxygen to the healing tissue under most conditions in the body","feeds back to accelerate earlier steps, amplifying its own generation","blocks all further clotting to prevent an excessive clot","immediately dissolves the clot it has just created"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thrombin exerts positive feedback on the cascade — activating factors and platelets that promote further prothrombin activator formation — so that once enough thrombin appears, the reaction accelerates explosively to seal the wound quickly. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-47"},{"q":"Which event is considered the rate-limiting step of blood coagulation?","choices":["Cleavage of fibrinogen by thrombin into circulating fibrin monomers","Aggregation of platelets into the initial soft plug that forms over the wounded vessel","Cross-linking of fibrin strands by activated fibrin-stabilizing factor","Formation of prothrombin activator, which then converts prothrombin to thrombin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: generating prothrombin activator is the rate-limiting step; it converts prothrombin to thrombin in the presence of calcium. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 40"},{"q":"Thrombin is central to coagulation because it...","choices":["acts only on platelets and has no effect on plasma clotting proteins","converts fibrinogen to fibrin and also activates factors that accelerate its own formation","binds circulating vitamin K directly so that new clotting factors can then be manufactured","directly digests fibrin strands to keep the growing clot from spreading"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thrombin cleaves fibrinogen into fibrin and additionally activates factors (and platelets) upstream, creating positive feedback that accelerates further thrombin generation and rapidly amplifies the clot. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-47"},{"q":"Immediately after a vessel is cut, the first hemostatic response is...","choices":["conversion of prothrombin to thrombin throughout the plasma","fibrin cross-linking that seals the wound within seconds","release of erythropoietin to replace the lost red cells","vascular spasm that constricts the vessel and limits blood loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The earliest response is immediate vascular spasm (myogenic, neural, and local factors), which narrows the vessel and reduces blood loss even before the platelet plug and coagulation cascade take over. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 34-40"},{"q":"Place the phases of hemostasis in their correct order.","choices":["Vascular spasm, then platelet plug, then coagulation and clot formation","Clot retraction, then platelet plug, then vascular spasm","Coagulation first, then vascular spasm, and finally the platelet plug","Platelet plug, then fibrinolysis, then vascular spasm during this particular process"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemostasis proceeds as immediate vascular spasm, followed by formation of the platelet plug (primary hemostasis), and then the coagulation cascade producing a fibrin clot (secondary hemostasis), which is later remodeled and dissolved. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 34-48"},{"q":"The intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge at a common pathway that begins with the activation of...","choices":["fibrinogen, which is the first factor of the cascade","tissue factor, which is unique to the intrinsic pathway","plasminogen, which initiates clot formation","factor X, leading to prothrombin activator formation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both initiating pathways lead to activation of factor X; activated factor X (with factor V, calcium, and phospholipid) forms prothrombin activator, which converts prothrombin to thrombin. This shared final sequence is the common pathway. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 44-47"},{"q":"The extrinsic pathway of coagulation is triggered when...","choices":["tissue factor from damaged cells outside the vessel contacts the blood","platelets release erythropoietin at the site of injury","plasmin begins to break down an existing fibrin clot during this process","blood contacts negatively charged collagen within the vessel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The extrinsic (tissue factor) pathway begins when trauma exposes blood to tissue factor released by damaged extravascular cells; this rapidly activates the cascade toward prothrombin activator, and it is the faster of the two initiating pathways. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 44-46"},{"q":"Calcium ions are required at multiple steps of coagulation because they...","choices":["dissolve the clot completely once the healing process is fully complete","are needed for clotting factors to assemble on phospholipid surfaces","directly cleave fibrinogen into fibrin strands","are the enzyme that converts prothrombin to thrombin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calcium (factor IV) bridges vitamin K-dependent clotting factors to the negatively charged phospholipid surfaces of platelets, allowing the reaction complexes to assemble; without calcium, the cascade cannot proceed, which is why calcium chelation prevents clotting in a blood tube. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-47"},{"q":"Intact, undamaged endothelium helps prevent unwanted clotting by...","choices":["actively cross-linking any fibrin that forms nearby during this process in the tissues","absorbing all calcium out of the surrounding plasma","continuously releasing thrombin into the passing blood","presenting a smooth, negatively charged surface that repels platelets and factors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Healthy endothelium is smooth and carries a negative surface charge that repels platelets and clotting factors, and it secretes antithrombotic substances; this keeps blood fluid until injury exposes the underlying tissue and triggers hemostasis. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 34, 48"},{"q":"The intrinsic pathway of coagulation is initiated when blood contacts...","choices":["erythropoietin that is secreted by the injured kidney into the blood","bile salts entering the bloodstream from the gut","a negatively charged surface such as exposed subendothelial collagen","tissue factor released from damaged extravascular cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The intrinsic (contact) pathway is triggered when blood is exposed to negatively charged surfaces like subendothelial collagen, which activates factor XII and begins the cascade using components already present in the blood. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 44-47"},{"q":"The extrinsic pathway is triggered when tissue factor is exposed. The extrinsic and intrinsic pathways then converge by...","choices":["releasing thromboxane A2 to recruit additional nearby platelets","activating factor X, which assembles prothrombin activator","activating factor XII on contact with subendothelial collagen","cleaving fibrinogen directly without first forming thrombin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: both pathways activate factor X, forming prothrombin activator in the common pathway. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 44-47"},{"q":"Von Willebrand factor contributes to hemostasis mainly by...","choices":["helping platelets adhere to the exposed vessel wall at the injury site","dissolving the fibrin meshwork after the wound heals","stimulating the marrow to produce more platelets under most normal conditions","converting prothrombin directly into active thrombin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Von Willebrand factor bridges platelet surface receptors to exposed subendothelial collagen, anchoring platelets at the site of injury; it also stabilizes factor VIII in the circulation, so its deficiency causes a bleeding tendency. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 33-34"}]};
